{{also|Morgen}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|nl|morgen}} and {{bor|en|de|Morgen}}, both literally "morning", probably originally indicated the amount of land that can be ploughed by a team of oxen in a morning.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|morgen|morgens}}

# {{lb|en|historical}} A unit of measurement of land in the [[Netherlands]] and the Dutch colonies and parts of the [[United States]], where it was equivalent two about two [[acre]]s; and in [[Denmark]], [[Norway]] and parts of [[Germany]], where it was equivalent to about two-thirds of an [[acre]]. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#*{{quote-book|1969|{{w|Doris Lessing}}|The Four-Gated City|edition=1993|publisher=HarperCollins|page=68|
|passage=‘All my life spent hating a poor little tyrant on a few '''morgen''' of poor soil, and he'd never known anything else.’}}

===Further reading===
* {{pedia}}

===Anagrams===
* {{anagrams|en|Monger|monger}}

